wise initializer follows declaration order: id, title, body, tags, pinned. Note: the
 struct doesn't have a public init. Since the test is @testable import, that's fine
. But parseNote is in the same module so it's fine.

One subtle point: `split` returns [Substring]; the tuple destructuring works fine
.

Another subtle point: with omittingEmptySubsequences: false, for an empty text ""
 you get [""], which is 1 part → malformed. Good.

Should pinned be strictly "1"/"0"? The spec says pinned flag ("1" or "0"). Rust uses
 `parts[2] == "1"` — anything other than "1" is false. Match Rust: pinned: pinned
Flag == "1". The spec doesn't say to throw on an invalid pinned flag, just 4 parts
. Keep it consistent with Rust.

